::ENG4_U01_L03_GP_Q1::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: A community college review found that students with reliable evening transportation were more likely to attend tutoring sessions, library workshops, and study groups after work. The report did not claim that transportation alone caused higher grades. Instead, it concluded that dependable transit expanded students' access to academic support services. The strongest evidence came from attendance records showing that late-afternoon workshop participation increased after the college added a shuttle route to nearby bus stops. Which paraphrase is most accurate? {
~A. The shuttle caused students to earn higher grades.#This overstates the source.
=B. The shuttle appeared to expand access to support services because workshop attendance increased after the route was added.#Correct. This preserves the evidence and avoids exaggeration.
~C. Students stopped needing tutoring once the shuttle was added.#The passage does not say this.
~D. Transportation was the only barrier students faced.#The passage says other barriers should be studied.
}
::ENG4_U01_L03_GP_Q2::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: A community college review found that students with reliable evening transportation were more likely to attend tutoring sessions, library workshops, and study groups after work. Which word best describes evidence that directly supports a claim? {
=A. Relevant#Correct. Relevant evidence directly connects to a claim.
~B. Decorative#Decorative does not describe evidence quality.
~C. Inflated#Inflated suggests exaggeration.
~D. Vague#Vague evidence is not directly useful.
}
::ENG4_U01_L03_GP_Q3::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The report did not claim that transportation alone caused higher grades. Instead, it concluded that dependable transit expanded students' access to academic support services. Which claim is supported by the passage? {
~A. Transportation alone caused higher grades.#The passage explicitly rejects this claim.
~B. Tutoring is unnecessary when transit improves.#The passage does not say this.
=C. Dependable transit can expand students' access to academic support services.#Correct. This matches the report's conclusion.
~D. Childcare is the main reason students miss workshops.#The passage lists childcare as another barrier to study.
}
::ENG4_U01_L03_GP_Q4::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: The strongest evidence came from attendance records showing that late-afternoon workshop participation increased after the college added a shuttle route to nearby bus stops. Which oral explanation is most logically organized? {
~A. The shuttle was useful, and I think transportation is important, and students need things.#This is vague and loosely organized.
=B. The attendance records are relevant because workshop participation increased after the shuttle route was added, which connects transit access to academic support use.#Correct. This states evidence and explains relevance.
~C. The report had evidence and buses and workshops, so it is credible.#This does not clearly explain relevance.
~D. Students probably liked the shuttle because it was nearby.#This speculates beyond the passage.
}
::ENG4_U01_L03_GP_Q5::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The review recommended continuing the shuttle program while also studying other barriers, including childcare, course scheduling, and internet access. What must an accurate paraphrase preserve? {
~A. The idea that transportation is the only barrier#This contradicts the passage.
~B. The idea that the shuttle should be canceled#The passage recommends continuing it.
~C. The claim that internet access is unrelated#The passage includes internet access as another barrier.
=D. The recommendation to continue the shuttle while studying other barriers#Correct. This preserves both parts of the recommendation.
}
